WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:03.610 --> 00:00:06.650
SARAH O'NEILL: With every opioid medication or narcotic pain

00:00:06.650 --> 00:00:08.870
medication that we dispense in the pharmacy,

00:00:08.870 --> 00:00:12.680
we do include the Massachusetts Department of Public Health's handout

00:00:12.680 --> 00:00:15.290
which goes over everything that anybody should

00:00:15.290 --> 00:00:21.050
know about pain medication and the risk of abuse and addiction,

00:00:21.050 --> 00:00:25.880
and how to properly dispose of it, and the importance of having

00:00:25.880 --> 00:00:29.660
a nasal naloxone kit in addition, because anybody

00:00:29.660 --> 00:00:32.390
is at risk of an overdose, not just somebody who is an addict.

00:00:32.390 --> 00:00:34.730
Even if you're just starting it for the first time,

00:00:34.730 --> 00:00:39.410
you might be sensitive to it, and you might be at risk for an overdose

00:00:39.410 --> 00:00:41.690
if you take it not as prescribed.

00:00:41.690 --> 00:00:45.770
So that is one part of the counseling that we do with all the opioids.

00:00:45.770 --> 00:00:49.520
But more specifically the side effects are very important to know about.

00:00:49.520 --> 00:00:52.580
And almost every prescription for an opioid

00:00:52.580 --> 00:00:55.670
is dispensed with a couple other medications

00:00:55.670 --> 00:00:58.490
as well to help decrease those side effects.

00:00:58.490 --> 00:01:01.160
And the major side effect is constipation.

00:01:01.160 --> 00:01:04.640
So almost every prescription, for example for Oxycodone,

00:01:04.640 --> 00:01:07.460
that comes into the pharmacy is also paired

00:01:07.460 --> 00:01:10.190
with a prescription for a stool softener,

00:01:10.190 --> 00:01:13.670
like Colace, where the generic name is Docusate

00:01:13.670 --> 00:01:16.640
in addition to a laxative called Senna.

00:01:16.640 --> 00:01:19.400
Taking both Senna and Docusate with Oxycodone

00:01:19.400 --> 00:01:23.240
is very important to prevent the constipation side

00:01:23.240 --> 00:01:25.490
effect from all the opioid medications.

00:01:25.490 --> 00:01:27.780
So every time you're taking--

00:01:27.780 --> 00:01:30.150
for as long as you're taking the pain medication,

00:01:30.150 --> 00:01:34.430
we always recommend that you take the bowel regimen medication in addition

00:01:34.430 --> 00:01:36.500
to it to prevent the constipation.

00:01:36.500 --> 00:01:38.840
In addition to the constipation side effects,

00:01:38.840 --> 00:01:41.220
the major side effect is drowsiness.

00:01:41.220 --> 00:01:45.020
So adding any other medication or alcohol

00:01:45.020 --> 00:01:48.200
that causes drowsiness in addition to this medication,

00:01:48.200 --> 00:01:49.640
could be very dangerous.

00:01:49.640 --> 00:01:53.240
So we like to reiterate that no other medications that

00:01:53.240 --> 00:01:57.660
could cause drowsiness, such as Benzodiazepines, like Lorazepam,

00:01:57.660 --> 00:02:02.690
or Ativan, Xanax, things like that, should be avoided

00:02:02.690 --> 00:02:04.790
when you're taking these medications.

00:02:04.790 --> 00:02:09.650
We also recommend that you don't drive, because it's very powerful medication

00:02:09.650 --> 00:02:12.830
that will make you very sleepy and it could potentially

00:02:12.830 --> 00:02:16.930
make it so that you're not in the right state of mind to be driving.

00:02:16.930 --> 00:02:20.650
And we don't recommend operating any heavy machinery,

00:02:20.650 --> 00:02:22.550
anything similar to driving.

00:02:22.550 --> 00:02:27.500
You should basically be at home and taking your pain medication rather

00:02:27.500 --> 00:02:31.370
than being out and about and going about your regular day because

00:02:31.370 --> 00:02:35.260
of the significant drowsiness.

